# Sharek Backend API - REST Client guide
#
# How to use:
# 1. Install the VS Code extension: REST Client.
# 2. Run the backend locally. Default URL is http://localhost:4000.
# 3. Open this file and click "Send Request" above any request.
# 4. Start with Health, then Register, Verify Email, Login, then protected
#    requests.
# 5. Do not run every request blindly. Some requests are optional, like
#    GitHub disconnect, admin role assignment, refresh, and logout.
#
# Important:
# - Public endpoint: no Authorization header needed.
# - Protected endpoint: must send "Authorization: Bearer <accessToken>".
# - Values at the top are frontend/dev placeholders. Replace emails, OTPs,
#   OAuth codes, and user ids with real values from your current environment.
# - The login response returns:
#   {
#     "user": { ... },
#     "tokens": {
#       "accessToken": "...",
#       "refreshToken": "...",
#       "expiresAt": "...",
#       "refreshExpiresAt": "..."
#     }
#   }
# - If register returns EMAIL_TAKEN, the user already exists. Run Login instead.
# - Email/password register does not return tokens anymore. It sends a 6-digit
#   OTP by SMTP/email. In local dev with no SMTP config, check the backend logs.
#   Then call /auth/verify-email to activate the account and receive tokens.
# - Keep Logout near the end. Logout revokes the session, so later protected
#   requests with the same token will return 401.
# - Refresh creates a new access token for the same session. After refresh,
#   the old login access token is no longer the active token.
#
# Test account rules:
# - You can register/login with email/password, Google, or GitHub.
# - Direct Google/GitHub auth creates or logs into one Share-k user. If the
#   provider email matches an existing user, the provider is linked to that user.
# - The role in /auth/google/start or /auth/github/start is used only when a
#   new user is created. Existing users keep their current role.
# - You can create one owner user and one contributor user with different
#   emails in this file.
# - The same real GitHub account cannot be connected to both users at the same
#   time. Use two GitHub accounts, disconnect first, or delete local test rows.
# - Direct GitHub auth also stores/refreshes the connected GitHub account token.
# - Contributor GitHub connect is the main skill-evidence flow for public and
#   private repositories.
# - Owner project import does not require connecting the owner GitHub account.
#   The owner can paste a public GitHub repo URL/fullName in Step 11.
#
# Implemented/fixed endpoint status:
# - [DONE] GET /health
# - [DONE] POST /auth/register
# - [DONE] GET /auth/username-availability
# - [DONE] POST /auth/verify-email
# - [DONE] POST /auth/verify-email/resend
# - [DONE] POST /auth/login
# - [DONE] POST /auth/forgot-password
# - [DONE] POST /auth/reset-password
# - [DONE] GET /auth/google/start
# - [DONE] GET /auth/google/callback
# - [DONE] POST /auth/google/callback
# - [DONE] GET /auth/github/start
# - [DONE] GET /auth/github/callback
# - [DONE] POST /auth/github/callback
# - [DONE] GET /auth/me
# - [DONE] GET /github/oauth/start
# - [DONE] GET /github/oauth/callback
# - [DONE] POST /github/oauth/callback
# - [DONE] GET /github/account
# - [DONE] GET /github/repositories
# - [DONE] GET /github/readme
# - [DONE] GET /github/repository/description
# - [DONE] GET /github/repository/statistics
# - [DONE] GET /github/repository/contribution-activity
# - [DONE] GET /github/repository/commit-signals
# - [DONE] POST /projects/import/github
# - [DONE] POST /contributors/profiles/me/ensure
# - [DONE] GET /contributors/profiles/:username
# - [DONE] POST /skill-profiles/me/generations
# - [DONE] GET /skill-profiles/me/generations/:generationId
# - [DONE] DELETE /github/account
# - [DONE] PATCH /auth/users/:id/role
# - [DONE] POST /auth/refresh
# - [DONE] POST /auth/logout
#
# GitHub flow status:
# - [DONE] Register/login with Share-k auth.
# - [DONE] Register/login directly with Google or GitHub social auth.
# - [DONE] Connect GitHub after login through OAuth.
# - [DONE] List connected GitHub repositories. Contributors request GitHub
#   `repo` consent so this can include public and private repositories for
#   skill evidence. Owners/admins keep lighter public repository consent.
# - [DONE] Import a GitHub project with README, languages, repository stats,
#   contribution activity, and recent commit signals where GitHub exposes them.
# - [MANUAL] Real browser OAuth still needs a real GitHub OAuth app/code.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:4000
@ownerEmail = owner@example.com
@contributorEmail = contributor@example.com
@adminEmail = admin@example.com
@ownerUsername = sharek-owner
@contributorUsername = sharek-contributor
@password = Password123!
@ownerOtp = replace-with-owner-email-otp
@contributorOtp = replace-with-contributor-email-otp
@passwordResetOtp = replace-with-password-reset-otp
@githubRepoFullName = openai/openai-node
@githubRepoUrl = https://github.com/openai/openai-node
@userIdToPromote = replace-with-real-user-id


### 01 - DONE - Health check
# What it does:
# Checks if the backend server is alive.
#
# Auth:
# Public.
#
# Expected success response:
# {
#   "message": "Service is healthy",
#   "status": "ok"
# }
GET {{baseUrl}}/health


### 02 - DONE - Register owner user
# @name ownerRegister
# What it does:
# Creates a new pending user with role "owner" and sends an email OTP.
#
# Auth:
# Public.
#
# Body rules:
# - email must be valid.
# - password must be at least 8 characters.
# - username must be unique, not reserved, and match lowercase URL-safe rules.
# - role must be "owner" or "contributor".
# - preferredLanguage must be "en" or "ar".
#
# Expected success response:
# user + emailVerificationRequired + verificationExpiresAt.
# No tokens are returned until Step 02B verifies the OTP.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{ownerEmail}}",
  "password": "{{password}}",
  "username": "{{ownerUsername}}",
  "firstName": "Sharek",
  "lastName": "Owner",
  "role": "owner",
  "preferredLanguage": "en"
}


### 02A - DONE - Check username availability
# @name usernameAvailability
# What it does:
# Checks the same username policy used by POST /auth/register.
#
# Auth:
# Public.
#
# Expected success response:
# {
#   "available": true,
#   "suggestion": null,
#   "reason": null
# }
GET {{baseUrl}}/auth/username-availability?username={{ownerUsername}}


### 02B - DONE - Verify owner email OTP
# @name ownerVerifyEmail
# What it does:
# Activates the pending owner account and returns user + tokens.
#
# Where to find the code:
# - In real SMTP/Gmail config, check the user's email inbox.
# - In local dev without SMTP config, check backend logs for the OTP.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/verify-email
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{ownerEmail}}",
  "code": "{{ownerOtp}}"
}


### 03 - DONE - Register contributor user
# @name contributorRegister
# What it does:
# Creates a pending contributor account and sends an email OTP.
#
# Auth:
# Public.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{contributorEmail}}",
  "password": "{{password}}",
  "username": "{{contributorUsername}}",
  "firstName": "Sharek",
  "lastName": "Contributor",
  "role": "contributor",
  "preferredLanguage": "ar"
}


### 03B - DONE - Verify contributor email OTP
# @name contributorVerifyEmail
# What it does:
# Activates the pending contributor account and returns user + tokens.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/verify-email
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{contributorEmail}}",
  "code": "{{contributorOtp}}"
}


### 03C - DONE - Resend email OTP
# What it does:
# Sends a fresh OTP for a pending account. The latest OTP is the valid one.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/verify-email/resend
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{ownerEmail}}"
}


### 03D - DONE - Request password reset OTP
# What it does:
# Creates a password-reset OTP for an existing account and sends it by email.
# In local development without SMTP, inspect the backend logs for the OTP.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/forgot-password
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{ownerEmail}}"
}


### 03E - DONE - Reset password with OTP
# What it does:
# Replaces the account password using the latest valid six-digit reset OTP.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/reset-password
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{ownerEmail}}",
  "code": "{{passwordResetOtp}}",
  "newPassword": "NewPassword123!"
}


### 04 - DONE - Login as owner
# @name ownerLogin
# What it does:
# Logs in with email/password and returns user + tokens.
# Login works only after Step 02B activates the account.
#
# Auth:
# Public.
#
# Frontend usage:
# - Store accessToken for API calls.
# - Store refreshToken if the app supports session refresh.
# - Send accessToken as:
#   Authorization: Bearer <accessToken>
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{ownerEmail}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}


### 04B - DONE - Login as contributor
# @name contributorLogin
# What it does:
# Logs in with the contributor account and returns user + tokens.
#
# Why this exists:
# The GitHub evidence flow below is contributor-first. Contributors connect
# GitHub so Share-k can analyze public and private repositories for skills.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{contributorEmail}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}


### 04C - DONE - Start Google sign up/sign in
# @name googleAuthStart
# What it does:
# Starts direct Google social auth. Use this for either signup or signin.
#
# Auth:
# Public.
#
# Query:
# - role: "owner" or "contributor". This is used only if a new Share-k user
#   must be created. Existing users keep their saved role.
#
# Manual test:
# 1. Click "Send Request".
# 2. Open authorizationUrl in the browser.
# 3. Approve Google.
# 4. Google redirects to /auth/google/callback with code/state.
# 5. Use that real code/state in Step 04D if your browser does not show JSON.
GET {{baseUrl}}/auth/google/start?role=contributor


### 04C-B - DONE - Google browser callback to frontend
# What it does:
# Forwards the real Google provider callback to the frontend callback page.
#
# Manual only:
# Use the one-time code and state returned by Google. Placeholder values will
# not complete authentication.
GET {{baseUrl}}/auth/google/callback?code=replace-with-google-code&state=replace-with-google-state


### 04D - DONE - Complete Google sign up/sign in from frontend
# What it does:
# Exchanges the real Google OAuth code/state and returns the normal Share-k
# AuthSessionDto: user + accessToken + refreshToken.
#
# Important:
# code is one-time. If this fails once, run Step 04C again.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/google/callback
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "code": "replace-with-google-code",
  "state": "{{googleAuthStart.response.body.$.state}}"
}


### 04E - DONE - Start GitHub sign up/sign in
# @name githubAuthStart
# What it does:
# Starts direct GitHub social auth. This is different from Step 06:
# - This endpoint logs a user in or creates a Share-k account.
# - Step 06 connects GitHub for an already logged-in user.
#
# Auth:
# Public.
#
# Role behavior:
# - role is used only when a new Share-k user must be created.
# - this social auth endpoint requests only GitHub identity scope:
#   `read:user user:email`.
# - it does not request private repository access and does not complete the
#   repository-evidence connection.
# - after signup/login, contributors should use Step 06 to explicitly connect
#   GitHub repositories from profile/onboarding.
GET {{baseUrl}}/auth/github/start?role=contributor


### 04E-B - DONE - GitHub identity browser callback to frontend
# What it does:
# Forwards the real GitHub identity callback to the frontend callback page.
#
# Manual only:
# Use the one-time code and state returned by GitHub.
GET {{baseUrl}}/auth/github/callback?code=replace-with-github-code&state=replace-with-github-state


### 04F - DONE - Complete GitHub sign up/sign in from frontend
# What it does:
# Exchanges the real GitHub OAuth code/state and returns the normal Share-k
# AuthSessionDto. Direct GitHub auth only proves identity and links the auth
# provider account. Repository access is a separate consent step in Step 06.
#
# Important:
# code is one-time. If this fails once, run Step 04E again.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/github/callback
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "code": "replace-with-github-code",
  "state": "{{githubAuthStart.response.body.$.state}}"
}


### 05 - DONE - Get current logged-in user
# What it does:
# Returns the profile of the user connected to the access token.
#
# Auth:
# Protected. Uses the access token from "04 - Login as owner".
#
# Expected success response:
# AuthUserDto:
# {
#   "id": "...",
#   "email": "...",
#   "firstName": "...",
#   "lastName": "...",
#   "avatarUrl": null,
#   "role": "owner",
#   "status": "active",
#   "preferredLanguage": "en",
#   "createdAt": "...",
#   "updatedAt": "...",
#   "lastLoginAt": "..."
# }
GET {{baseUrl}}/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerLogin.response.body.$.tokens.accessToken}}


### 06 - DONE - Start GitHub OAuth connection
# What it does:
# Starts GitHub connect flow for the logged-in contributor.
#
# Auth:
# Protected.
#
# Expected success response:
# {
#   "authorizationUrl": "https://github.com/login/oauth/authorize?...",
#   "state": "...",
#   "expiresAt": "..."
# }
#
# Frontend usage:
# Redirect the browser to authorizationUrl.
#
# Role behavior:
# - Contributor OAuth requests GitHub `repo` scope so Share-k can fetch public
#   and private repositories for skill evidence after explicit consent.
# - Owner/admin OAuth requests lighter public repository consent. This is now
#   optional because owner project import can use a public repo URL/fullName.
#
# Manual test:
# 1. Click "Send Request" for this request.
# 2. Copy authorizationUrl from the response.
# 3. Open that authorizationUrl in your browser.
# 4. Approve access on GitHub.
# 5. GitHub should redirect to /auth/github/callback/repository, which forwards
#    the browser to the frontend /auth/callback route with real code/state.
# 6. The frontend then calls Step 08 to finish the connection. If Step 08 does
#    not succeed, the GitHub account is not connected yet.
#
# If the callback returns GITHUB_ACCOUNT_TAKEN:
# - You approved GitHub with an account already linked to another Share-k user
#   in this local database.
# - Step 09 will still return GITHUB_ACCOUNT_NOT_CONNECTED for the current
#   contributor token because this contributor did not receive the connection.
# - Fix by logging in as the Share-k user that already owns that GitHub account
#   and running Step 14 to disconnect it, or use a different GitHub account.
GET {{baseUrl}}/github/oauth/start
Authorization: Bearer {{contributorLogin.response.body.$.tokens.accessToken}}


### 06B - DONE - GitHub repository provider callback
# What it does:
# Completes the repository OAuth flow directly from a provider redirect.
# Most frontend flows use the browser callback below and then POST the values
# from the frontend instead.
#
# Manual only:
# Use the one-time code and state returned by GitHub.
GET {{baseUrl}}/github/oauth/callback?code=replace-with-github-code&state=replace-with-oauth-state


### 07 - DONE - GitHub OAuth browser redirect to frontend
# What it does:
# This is the redirect endpoint GitHub can call after the user approves
# repository access. It forwards the browser to the frontend /auth/callback
# page with code/state.
#
# Auth:
# Public. The security check is the OAuth state value.
#
# Frontend note:
# In a real browser flow, GitHub redirects to this URL with code and state,
# then the frontend calls Step 08.
#
# Important:
# Do not click this request with the placeholder values below. It will not
# connect GitHub by itself. Use the browser redirect from Step 06 and let the
# frontend call Step 08.
#
# Expected response:
# 302 redirect to http://localhost:3001/auth/callback?provider=github&code=...
GET {{baseUrl}}/auth/github/callback/repository?code=replace-with-github-code&state=replace-with-oauth-state


### 08 - DONE - GitHub OAuth callback from frontend
# What it does:
# Connects the GitHub account when the frontend receives code/state and sends
# them to the backend.
#
# Auth:
# Public. The security check is the OAuth state value.
#
# Body:
# - code: value returned by GitHub.
# - state: value returned by "06 - Start GitHub OAuth connection".
#
# Important:
# Do not use placeholder values. The GitHub code is one-time and expires fast.
# If this request fails once, run Step 06 again and use the new authorizationUrl.
POST {{baseUrl}}/github/oauth/callback
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "code": "replace-with-github-code",
  "state": "replace-with-oauth-state"
}


### 09 - DONE - Get connected GitHub account
# What it does:
# Returns the GitHub account connected to the logged-in user.
#
# Auth:
# Protected.
#
# If this returns GITHUB_ACCOUNT_NOT_CONNECTED:
# - Step 07/08 did not finish successfully.
# - Or Step 06 was started with a different Share-k user token.
# - Or the database was reset/restarted after connecting.
# - Or Step 07/08 returned GITHUB_ACCOUNT_TAKEN, meaning the GitHub account is
#   already linked to another Share-k user and was not connected to this token.
# Run Step 06 again, open authorizationUrl in browser, approve GitHub, confirm
# the callback returns GitHubAccountDto, then run Step 09 again.
#
# Expected success response:
# GitHubAccountDto:
# {
#   "id": "...",
#   "githubId": "...",
#   "username": "...",
#   "avatarUrl": "...",
#   "profileUrl": "...",
#   "ingestionStatus": "pending|in_progress|completed|failed",
#   "connectedAt": "...",
#   "lastSyncedAt": null
# }
GET {{baseUrl}}/github/account
Authorization: Bearer {{contributorLogin.response.body.$.tokens.accessToken}}


### 10 - DONE - List GitHub repositories
# What it does:
# Lists repositories available from the connected contributor GitHub account.
# With contributor `repo` consent, this can include public and private repos.
#
# Auth:
# Protected.
#
# If this fails:
# - GITHUB_ACCOUNT_NOT_CONNECTED means run steps 06 and 07/08 first.
# - GITHUB_TOKEN_DECRYPT_FAILED means the local GITHUB_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY
#   changed after connecting GitHub. Run step 14 if possible, then reconnect
#   GitHub with steps 06 and 07/08.
# - GITHUB_API_REQUEST_FAILED usually means the GitHub token is invalid,
#   expired, missing scope, or GitHub rejected the request. Reconnect GitHub
#   and verify the contributor OAuth screen includes repo access.
# - GITHUB_REPOSITORY_LIST_INVALID_RESPONSE means GitHub did not return the
#   expected repository array. Check backend logs and reconnect GitHub.
#
# Query:
# - page: starts at 1.
# - perPage: defaults to 12 and is capped at 50.
#
# Expected success response:
# Paginated GitHubRepositoryDto picker data:
# {
#   "items": [ ... ],
#   "page": 1,
#   "perPage": 12,
#   "hasNextPage": false
# }
# Each item includes identity, description, URL, visibility, default branch,
# languages, topics, stars, forks, open issues, watchers, pushedAt, and
# updatedAt.
GET {{baseUrl}}/github/repositories?page=1&perPage=12
Authorization: Bearer {{contributorLogin.response.body.$.tokens.accessToken}}


### 10B - DONE - Get repository README
# What it does:
# Returns raw README content for a connected GitHub repository, if available.
GET {{baseUrl}}/github/readme?fullName={{githubRepoFullName}}
Authorization: Bearer {{contributorLogin.response.body.$.tokens.accessToken}}


### 10C - DONE - Get repository description
# What it does:
# Returns the GitHub repository description.
GET {{baseUrl}}/github/repository/description?fullName={{githubRepoFullName}}
Authorization: Bearer {{contributorLogin.response.body.$.tokens.accessToken}}


### 10D - DONE - Get repository statistics
# What it does:
# Returns normalized repo counters, contribution activity, and commit signals.
GET {{baseUrl}}/github/repository/statistics?fullName={{githubRepoFullName}}
Authorization: Bearer {{contributorLogin.response.body.$.tokens.accessToken}}


### 10E - DONE - Get repository contribution activity
# What it does:
# Returns contributor and weekly commit activity when GitHub exposes it.
GET {{baseUrl}}/github/repository/contribution-activity?fullName={{githubRepoFullName}}
Authorization: Bearer {{contributorLogin.response.body.$.tokens.accessToken}}


### 10F - DONE - Get repository commit signals
# What it does:
# Returns recent commit headlines/authors/dates. Optional author filters the
# returned recent commits by author text.
GET {{baseUrl}}/github/repository/commit-signals?fullName={{githubRepoFullName}}
Authorization: Bearer {{contributorLogin.response.body.$.tokens.accessToken}}


### 11 - DONE - Import GitHub project
# What it does:
# Imports or refreshes a project from a public GitHub repository full name or URL.
#
# Auth:
# Protected. Role must be "owner" or "admin".
#
# Body rules:
# Send either:
# - fullName: GitHub repo full name in "owner/repository" format.
# - repoUrl: public GitHub repo URL, like "https://github.com/owner/repo".
#
# Important:
# Owner GitHub connect is not required for this step. The backend uses GitHub's
# public repository API. Private owner repositories are not imported by this MVP
# endpoint; the owner should choose a public project repo for contributors.
#
# Expected success response:
# ImportedProjectDto:
# {
#   "id": "...",
#   "ownerId": "...",
#   "title": "...",
#   "description": "...",
#   "githubRepoUrl": "...",
#   "githubRepoId": "...",
#   "languages": ...,
#   "tags": ...,
#   "technologies": ...,
#   "repoStatistics": {
#     "stars": 5,
#     "forks": 1,
#     "contributionActivity": {
#       "totalContributors": 3,
#       "totalCommits": 42,
#       "lastYearCommitCount": 20,
#       "unavailableReason": null
#     },
#     "commitSignals": {
#       "recentCommitCount": 30,
#       "authors": ["owner-login"],
#       "unavailableReason": null
#     }
#   },
#   "status": "draft",
#   "readmeContent": "...",
#   "createdAt": "...",
#   "updatedAt": "..."
# }
#
# Notes:
# - This owner import step is separate from the contributor skill-evidence
#   flow above.
# - Product direction: contributor GitHub is for skill evidence; owner project
#   import is a public repo selection by URL/fullName.
# - The backend also fetches README, contribution activity, and recent commit
#   signals for the normalized snapshot.
# - GitHub stats endpoints can return pending/unavailable. In that case import
#   still succeeds and repoStatistics contains an unavailableReason.
POST {{baseUrl}}/projects/import/github
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerLogin.response.body.$.tokens.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "repoUrl": "{{githubRepoUrl}}"
}


### 11B - DONE - Ensure my contributor profile
# What it does:
# Creates or returns the authenticated contributor profile. This is idempotent.
#
# Auth:
# Protected. Use a contributor token. Pending contributors are allowed during
# onboarding; owners and admins receive 403.
POST {{baseUrl}}/contributors/profiles/me/ensure
Authorization: Bearer {{contributorLogin.response.body.$.tokens.accessToken}}


### 11C - DONE - Get contributor profile by username
# What it does:
# Returns the authenticated view of a canonical contributor profile username.
#
# Auth:
# Protected.
GET {{baseUrl}}/contributors/profiles/{{contributorUsername}}
Authorization: Bearer {{contributorLogin.response.body.$.tokens.accessToken}}


### 12 - DONE - Start contributor skill profile generation
# @name skillProfileGeneration
# What it does:
# Starts selected-repository AI skill profiling for the logged-in contributor.
# The generated skills are saved as pending and do not qualify for tasks until
# admin review approves them.
# Selections must come from the connected account repository list. Public
# repositories that are not in that authenticated list are rejected.
#
# Auth:
# Protected. Use a contributor token with GitHub repository access connected.
#
# Expected success response:
# {
#   "generationId": "...",
#   "status": "queued",
#   "progress": {
#     "selectedRepositoryCount": 1,
#     "snapshottedRepositoryCount": 0
#   },
#   "skills": []
# }
POST {{baseUrl}}/skill-profiles/me/generations
Authorization: Bearer {{contributorLogin.response.body.$.tokens.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "repositories": [
    { "fullName": "{{githubRepoFullName}}" }
  ]
}


### 13 - DONE - Get contributor skill profile generation status
# What it does:
# Polls the generation status after starting selected-repository skill
# profiling.
# Terminal states:
# - pending_review: reviewable pending skills were created.
# - needs_more_evidence: no sufficiently attributable evidence was available.
# - failed: retries were exhausted; failureReason contains a safe message.
GET {{baseUrl}}/skill-profiles/me/generations/{{skillProfileGeneration.response.body.$.generationId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{contributorLogin.response.body.$.tokens.accessToken}}


### 14 - DONE - Disconnect GitHub account
# What it does:
# Removes the connected GitHub account/token for the logged-in contributor.
#
# Auth:
# Protected.
#
# Expected success response:
# {
#   "success": true
# }
#
# Important:
# Run this only when you really want to disconnect the test GitHub account.
# To disconnect an owner GitHub link instead, change the token to ownerLogin.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/github/account
Authorization: Bearer {{contributorLogin.response.body.$.tokens.accessToken}}


### 15 - DONE - Login as admin
# @name adminLogin
# What it does:
# Logs in as an existing admin user.
#
# Important:
# The register endpoint does not allow creating admin users directly.
# You need an admin user already created in the database.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{adminEmail}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}


### 16 - DONE - Assign user role
# What it does:
# Changes a user's role to owner, contributor, or admin.
#
# Auth:
# Protected. Role must be "admin".
#
# Important:
# - Replace @userIdToPromote at the top with a real user id.
# - This uses the token from "15 - Login as admin".
# - If you use an owner/contributor token, the backend should return 403.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/auth/users/{{userIdToPromote}}/role
Authorization: Bearer {{adminLogin.response.body.$.tokens.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "role": "admin"
}


### 17 - DONE - Refresh owner tokens
# @name ownerRefresh
# What it does:
# Takes a refresh token and returns a new accessToken + refreshToken.
#
# Auth:
# Public, but it needs a valid refreshToken in the body.
#
# Important:
# After running this request, the old access token from "04 - Login as owner"
# is replaced for this session. Use ownerRefresh.response.body.$.accessToken
# after this point.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{ownerLogin.response.body.$.tokens.refreshToken}}"
}


### 18 - DONE - Get current user with refreshed token
# What it does:
# Same as "05 - Get current logged-in user", but uses the refreshed accessToken.
GET {{baseUrl}}/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerRefresh.response.body.$.accessToken}}


### 19A - DONE - Logout owner session without refresh
# What it does:
# Revokes the owner session using the original login access token.
#
# Auth:
# Protected.
#
# Important:
# Use this request only if you did NOT run "17 - Refresh owner tokens".
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/logout
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerLogin.response.body.$.tokens.accessToken}}


### 19B - DONE - Logout owner session after refresh
# What it does:
# Revokes the owner session using the refreshed access token.
#
# Auth:
# Protected.
#
# Important:
# Use this request only if you DID run "17 - Refresh owner tokens".
# After logout, protected requests using this session return 401.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/logout
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerRefresh.response.body.$.accessToken}}
